"""
target_live_checker.py

Checks whether Target TCINs resolve to a live product page on target.com.
Built on the same approach as omsid_checker.py (Home Depot).

MODES
-----
"sitemap"   Downloads Target's published product sitemap
            (https://www.target.com/sitemap_pdp-index.xml.gz, listed in
            target.com/robots.txt), builds a {tcin: url} index, and looks each
            TCIN up. No product page is requested. The index is cached to disk.

"api"       Queries Target's RedSky product API (the JSON endpoint the
            target.com product page itself calls), one request per TCIN, with
            a product-page fallback when the API is inconclusive.

"combined"  (default) Sitemap first. Every TCIN NOT found in the sitemap is
            then checked with "api". This matters because Target's sitemap may
            list only the variation parent (VAP) page and not every variation
            child TCIN, so a sitemap miss alone does not prove an item is down.

STATUS VALUES
-------------
LIVE        Resolves to a published product page.
NOT_FOUND   Target reports no product for the TCIN (or not in sitemap, in
            sitemap-only mode).
UNKNOWN     No definitive answer (blocked, rate limited, errors).

SAME LIMITATION AS THE HD SCRIPT: LIVE means the TCIN resolves to a URL. It
does not mean the item is in stock or purchasable. Sitemaps commonly retain
retired pages. API-mode rows include price and an all-stores out-of-stock flag
where Target supplies them; use those for a purchasability read.

SETUP (PyCharm Terminal)
------------------------
    pip install requests pandas openpyxl

OUTPUT
------
An Excel workbook, target_live_check_<timestamp>.xlsx, saved in the same
folder as this script (or OUTPUT_FOLDER), and opened automatically.
  Results  one row per TCIN in input order; clickable URLs; color-coded status.
  Summary  counts by status, and by status + method.
"""

